WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.760
We ask a lot of questions
about our diet.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.080
What’s the right way to
treat a chronic illness?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.720
Fight off a virus,
lose weight?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.640
The problem is, we get
a lot of different answers.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.880
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.240
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.400
Let’s see if you know more about
basic nutrition than most doctors.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.200
Here’s our first story.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.360
A poor diet now outranks smoking
as the leading cause of death

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.080
on the planet, as well as
specifically in the United States.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.480
In the US, the #1 killer of Americans
is the American diet.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.480
So, if diet is humanity's #1 killer,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.480
then obviously it's the #1 thing
taught in medical school, right?

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.840
Sadly, medical students around the
world are poorly trained in nutrition.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.400
It's not that medical students aren't
interested in learning about it.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.360
Medical schools just
aren't teaching it.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.240
Without a solid foundation of clinical
nutrition knowledge and skills,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.640
physicians worldwide are generally
not equipped to even begin to have

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.840
an informed conversation about
nutrition with their patients.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.320
How bad is it? One study assessing
the clinical nutrition knowledge

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:21.400
of medical doctors found the majority
got 70% of the questions wrong.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.600
And they were multiple
choice questions,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.360
so they should have gotten about
a fifth right just by chance.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.400
And the wrong answers were not limited
to dificult or demanding questions.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.480
For example, less than half could
guess how many calories are in

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.800
fat, carbs and protein; only 1 in 10
knew the recommended protein intake;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.800
and only about 1 in 3 knew
what a healthy BMI was.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.480
I mean, this is like super
basic nutrition knowledge.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.160
And what's worse, not only did
the majority of medical doctors

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:57.720
get a failing grade, but 30% of those
who failed had a high self-perception

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.360
of their clinical nutrition expertise,
meaning not only were they clueless

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.160
about nutrition, they were also clueless
that they were clueless about nutrition,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.320
a particularly bad combination
given that doctors are trusted

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.240
and influential sources
of healthy eating advice.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.480
For those majority of consumers
who get information

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.000
from their personal health care
professional, 78% indicate making

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.600
a change in their eating habits
as a result of those conversations.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.640
So if everything the doctor knows they
read in some checkout aisle magazine,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.960
that's what the patients
are going to be following.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.240
Only a quarter of doctors
surveyed correctly identified

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.480
the American Heart Association's
recommended number

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.000
of fruit and vegetable servings
per day, and fewer still

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.280
were aware of the recommended
daily added sugar limits.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.760
So how are they going
to counsel patients on it?

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.320
Yet again, of the doctors who perceived
themselves as having high nutrition

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.960
knowledge, 93% couldn't answer those
two basic multiple-choice questions.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.760
Physicians with no genuine
expertise in, say, brain surgery

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:07.520
are neither likely to broadcast
detailed opinions on that topic,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.600
nor have their "expert
opinions" solicited by media.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.000
And most topical domains in
medicine enjoy such respect;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.280
we defer expert opinion and
commentary to actual experts.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:25.720
Not so with nutrition, where the
common knowledge that physicians

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.440
are generally ill trained in this area
is conjoined to routine invitations

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.560
to physicians for their expert
opinions on the matter.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.960
All too many are willing to provide
their opinions, absent any basis

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:43.000
for actual expertise, or worse, made
on the basis of bias and personal

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.520
preference, sometimes directly tethered
to personal gain such as diet book sales.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.360
That's one of the reasons all the
proceeds I receive from my books

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.280
are donated directly to charity.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.440
I didn't even want the appearance
of any conflicts of interest.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.520
In a culture that routinely fails to
distinguish expertise from mere opinion

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.760
or personal anecdote, we physicians
should be doing all we can

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.520
to establish relevant barriers to entry
for expert opinion on diet and nutrition,

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.520
as in all other matters of
genuine medical significance.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.000
I mean, we're not talking
celebrity gossip.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.840
Lives are at stake, and there
are entire industries devoted

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.160
to marketing messages
that may conspire

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.920
directly against well-informed
medical advice in this area.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:30.880
Medical education must
be brought up to date.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.280
For physicians to be ill trained
in the very area most impactful

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.520
on the rate of premature death
at the population level

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.360
is an absurd anachronism. The
mission of medicine is to protect,

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.040
defend, and advance
the human condition.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:50.120
That mission cannot be
fulfilled if diet is neglected.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.400
Maybe one place to
start is for physicians

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.640
and health care organizations
to collectively begin to emphasize

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.720
their seriousness about
nutrition in health care

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.080
by practicing what they at
least should be preaching.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.240
Is it appropriate to serve pizza and
soft drinks at a resident conference

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.760
while bemoaning the high
prevalence of obesity

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.280
and encouraging patients
to eat healthier?

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:14.360
A similarly poor example exists
in medical conferences,

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:16.760
including national meetings,
where some morning sessions

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:22.200
are accompanied by foods
such as donuts and sausage.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.000
About a decade ago, the American Heart
Association expressed concern

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.840
that their “2020 target of improving
cardiovascular health by 20 percent

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:36.000
by 2020 [would] not be reached
if current trends continue[d].”

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.440
By 2006, most people were
already not smoking,

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.760
and had nearly achieved
their goal for exercise.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:47.200
But when it came to healthy diet
score, only about 1 percent

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.960
got a 4 or 5 out of their
0 to 5 diet quality score,

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.520
and that’s with so-called
“ideal” criteria of like drinking

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.040
less than four and a half
cups of soda a week.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.200
In the last decade they
saw a bump up to like 1 percent

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:04.160
of Americans even reaching
those kinds of basic criteria.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.080
But given their aggressive goal
of improving that by 20 percent

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:13.840
by 2020, they hoped to turn that
1 percent into about 1.2 percent!

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:17.480
Okay, so how’d we do?
Let’s look at the 2019 update,

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:22.041
and it looks like we’ve slipped
down to as low as one in a thousand—

00:06:22.041 --> 00:06:26.200
you can’t even see the green anymore.
And American teens got a big fat zero.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:31.280
No wonder, perhaps, that “for all
mortality-based metrics, the U.S.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:37.000
rank declined … 27th or 28th
among 34 [industrialized] countries.”

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.320
“Citizens living in countries
with a substantially lower [GDP]

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.800
and health expenditure[s]
per capita . . . have lower

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.560
mortality rates than those
in the United States.”

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:49.280
Slovenia beat us by three
countries, coming in at 24th

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.040
in life expectancy to our 27th.
And more recently we seem to have

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.600
slipped to 43rd, even
though we spend trillions

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.520
on health care—more than anyone else.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:03.960
What’s the leading risk factor
for death in the United States?

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:12.080
What we eat. The standard American
diet is just to die for—literally.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.372
Those trillions in health care spending
aren’t addressing the root cause.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.600
“Approximately 80 percent of
chronic disease and premature death

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.040
could be prevented by not
smoking, being physically active,

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:27.320
and adhering to a
healthful dietary pattern.”

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.960
But what exactly is
meant by “healthy diet”?

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.800
Unfortunately, what we hear
about nutrition in the media

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.040
is often inconsistent and confusing.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:38.040
“There[’s a] pressure
within today's competitive

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:41.200
journalism market for sensationalism.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.200
There may even be a disincentive
to present the facts in … context…”

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:46.921
to sell more magazines.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.640
In fact, about three-quarters
of a century ago it was noted

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.800
that unfortunately, “the subject
of nutrition seems to have

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.720
a special appeal to the
credulous, the social zealot

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.600
and, in the commercial field,
the unscrupulous,” a combination

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.600
“calculated to strike
despair in the hearts

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:06.160
of the sober, objective scientist.”

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:09.080
“The most important [health care]
problem [we face may be]

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:13.160
our poor lifestyle choices
based on misinformation.”

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:16.320
It’s like the climate change deniers;
“healthy … dietary advice

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:20.400
[is] overshadowed by critics,
diet books, [industry interests],

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.000
and misguided information
in the media.”

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.375
Maybe what we need is
like an IPCC (Intergovernmental

00:08:26.375 --> 00:08:28.200
Panel on Climate Change) of nutrition.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:32.040
These days “[n]o single expert,
regardless of academic stature

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:36.080
or reputation, has the prominence
to overcome the obstacles

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.920
created by confusing media
messages and [effectively] deliver

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:43.960
the fundamental principles of
healthy living … to the public.”

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:48.960
However, what if there was
“a global coalition consisting

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.520
of a variety of nutrition experts,
who collectively represent

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:54.840
the views held by the
majority of scientists,

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.840
physicians, and health practitioners…”?

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:00.280
It could “serve as the guiding
resource of sound nutrition information

00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:03.240
for improved health and
prevention of disease.”

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:07.040
Boom! “The True Health
Initiative was conceived

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:11.800
for that very purpose.” A nonprofit
coalition of hundreds of experts

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.240
from dozens of countries
agreeing to a consensus statement

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:18.520
on the fundamentals of healthy living.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:22.040
Check out
https://www.truehealthinitiative.org/.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:26.320
Spoiler alert—the healthiest diet
is one generally comprised

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:30.433
mostly of minimally processed plants.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:32.960
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.200
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.960
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:43.440
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.920
If you hear about any graphs, charts,
graphics, images, or studies mentioned here,

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:51.520
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.880
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:58.880
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.960
My latest major book,
“How Not to Age” is out.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:03.640
Check it out from your
local public library.

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:07.520
I also wrote a
mini book on Ozempic.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:10.400
Available in softcover,
e-book, and audio versions.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.360
Check it out on NutritionFacts.org.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:15.080
And of course, all the proceeds
I receive of the sales

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:18.320
of all my books goes
directly to charity.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.680
NutritionFacts.org is a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.840
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:27.920
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.120
Everything on the
website is free.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:31.240
There are no ads,

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:33.020
no corporate sponsorships,
no kickbacks.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:36.480
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.000
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.640
as a tribute to
my grandmother,

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:47.760
whose own life was saved
with evidence-based nutrition.

